# --- Contract artifacts: what is and is not version controlled ---
#
# splits/      COMMITTED  — small JSON, and it is the paper's evidence of protocol
# results/     COMMITTED  — aggregate summaries, small
# predictions/ COMMITTED as .csv.gz only — 42 files x 2000 rows; gzip keeps it ~2MB
# probs/       COMMITTED  — 72 KB per rotation, ~2.1 MB for all 30. Small enough
#                           that committing them removes the B-to-A transfer step
#                           entirely and makes the study reproducible from a clone.
# tables/      COMMITTED  — the contract-4 deliverables D includes, plus the
#                           manifest that ties each printed number back to the
#                           artifacts it was computed from
# run_manifest.json
#              COMMITTED  — generated at results freeze; the single index tying
#                           splits, probabilities, decisions and tables to one
#                           commit, one environment and a set of checksums
# model.pt     IGNORED    — ~1.3 GB per rotation; archived out-of-band, and the
#                           bundle's meta.json carries its checksum
#
# The leading slash matters: an unanchored "predictions/" would also swallow
# contracts/examples/predictions/, and git does not descend into an excluded
# directory, so no later negation could bring the example files back.
/predictions/*.csv

# Model weights and caches
*.npy
*.npz
*.pt
!contracts/examples/**/*.npy
!/probs/**/*.npy
/checkpoints/
/checkpoints_*/

# Logs
*.log

# LaTeX build products. Three files under figures/ are COMMITTED and must not
# be caught here: figure1_hierarchy.tex (the drawing's source), its .pdf
# (contract §5 freezes it as the deliverable D includes), and the generated
# figure1_defs.tex — committed so the source compiles in an editor without
# running the rebuild first. Everything below is latexmk scratch.
*.aux
*.fdb_latexmk
*.fls
*.synctex.gz

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.venv/

.DS_Store

# Rendered preview of the delivered tables: a convenience, regenerated by
# python -m analysis.preview. Not committed -- latexmk stamps a creation date
# into every build, so it would churn on every run.
tables/preview.pdf
